.TH TRASH 1 "2026-11-25" "trash 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
trash \- move files and directories to the system trash
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trash
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.I FILE ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trash
moves each
.I FILE
to the trash of its volume instead of deleting it. Nothing is deleted
permanently: a target that cannot be moved to the trash is reported as an error
and left in place.
.PP
On Linux the freedesktop.org Trash specification is followed, so items appear
in file managers and in
.BR gio (1)
and
.B trash-cli
as usual. On macOS the system file manager moves the item, so Finder's
"Put Back" works. On Windows the Recycle Bin is used.
.PP
Failures are reported per item and the remaining targets are still processed.
The exit status is
.B 0
when every target was trashed and
.B 1
when any of them failed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Ignore missing targets. A target that does not exist is skipped silently and
does not change the exit status. This does not affect any other failure.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-R
Allow a directory to be trashed together with its contents.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dir
Allow an empty directory to be trashed without
.BR \-r .
A non-empty directory is still refused.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print
.B trashed 'FILE'
for every target that was moved.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the help text and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options. Every following argument is a target, even if it starts with a
dash.
.SH REFUSALS
These targets are refused with an error and nothing is moved:
.IP \(bu 2
a filesystem root;
.IP \(bu 2
the current directory;
.IP \(bu 2
a path whose last component is
.B .
or
.BR .. ;
.IP \(bu 2
the trash directory itself or one of its ancestors;
.IP \(bu 2
a mount point, or a directory that contains one;
.IP \(bu 2
a directory without
.B \-r
or, for a non-empty one,
.BR \-d ;
.IP \(bu 2
a target on a volume that has no usable trash directory;
.IP \(bu 2
on Windows, a path over 259 characters, a reserved device name, a
.B :
after the drive prefix, a last component ending in a dot or a space, a UNC
path, or an object larger than the Recycle Bin.
.SH DIFFERENCES FROM rm
.B trash
accepts only the
.BR rm (1)
flags that have a meaning without permanent deletion:
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-R ,
.BR \-d
and
.BR \-v .
Other
.B rm
flags, including
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-I ,
.B \-\-interactive ,
.B \-\-recursive
and the
.B \-\-preserve\-root
family, are rejected rather than ignored.
.PP
A symlink in the middle of a path is resolved the way
.BR rm (1)
resolves it, but a symlink given as the target is trashed as a link and never
dereferenced. Mount points are never trashed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every target was trashed, or was skipped because of
.BR \-f .
.TP
.B 1
At least one target failed, or the command line was invalid.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B trash notes.txt
Move one file to the trash.
.TP
.B trash -r old\-project
Move a directory tree to the trash.
.TP
.B trash -f \-v build.log
Move a log file, ignore a missing one, and print what was moved.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rm (1),
.BR gio (1)
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at the
.UR https://github.com/nunsez/trash/issues
issue tracker
.UE .
